 "verdict": [
  "Survives Holm-Bonferroni correction against: curated_agents_md, session_summary_extractive. Those are the only arm-vs-arm differences this corpus supports.",
  "SUBSET: against `lexical_recall` the memory arm wins ZERO episodes the baseline misses, while the baseline wins 2. That is stronger than 'not distinguished' - on this corpus its successes are a strict subset of the cheaper arm's, so there is no episode here that justifies the extra machinery.",
  "Families the memory arm never gets right: m-mutation-target, m-silent-import, m-single-shot-bench, m-unstaged-verify (4 of 15). An aggregate over families this uneven is describing a mixture, not a capability.",
  "Leave-one-family-out: removing `m-stale-claim` moves the headline difference from -0.0667 to -0.037 (swing 0.0297). THE EXIT CRITERION asks whether an aggregate hides a single easy family, and this is the number that answers it.",
  "No single family moves the headline much, so the aggregate is not carried by one easy case - which is the one reassuring thing in this report.",
  "COMPLEMENTARY with `linter_or_test`: it gets 10 episodes the memory arm misses, the memory arm gets 10 it misses, and the union reaches 0.8 against 0.4667 and 0.4667 alone. Two arms with similar aggregates are not the same system twice. The engineering conclusion is not that one wins - it is that a deployment should run both, and that comparing them head-to-head was the wrong question.",
  "The intervals here supersede the Wilson intervals in F1-F4 for arm-vs-arm claims: those treat paired data as unpaired, which is conservative but wrong. The Wilson figures remain correct for a single arm's own rate.",
  "All of this rests on one author-written corpus of 30 positive episodes. No correction repairs that; it is the dominant term in every uncertainty on this page."
 ]
